    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if i use .copy() it's basically making a whole new object in memory instead of just pointing to the old one? i noticed the warning specifically mentions \"a view of a DataFrame\". is that basically just a pointer thing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, `.copy()` creates a complete, independent duplicate of the data in memory, similar to how a regular variable copy works. The \"view\" mentioned in the warning is indeed like a lightweight reference or pointer that shares the same underlying memory as the original DataFrame, so changes \"bleed\" through. You essentially have a choice between modifying that shared memory (which triggers the warning) or allocating entirely new memory for your filtered result.",
      "source": "target",
