      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Being defensive with `.copy()` does have a measurable performance cost because it requires allocating new memory and copying all data, which can be expensive on very large datasets. However, this overhead is usually worth it when analyzing data, since saving yourself from subtle bugs is often more important than micro-optimizations. If you are working with massive datasets and strict performance is critical, you might consider processing your data in chunks or using methods that generate a new object without a verbose copy command. Ultimately, writing safer code is generally the recommended approach unless profiling shows specific bottlenecks elsewhere.",
